{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1201448-newbie-questions","id":1201448,"title":"Newbie Questions.","path":["Home","Onyx Path","Onyx Path Worlds","Scion"],"posts":[{"date":"2018-03-30T15:46:21-07:00","id":1201448,"title":"Newbie Questions.","content":"Hello! Alright, so cards on the table. This will be kindof rambly and kindof alot.<br /> <br /> I got interested in Scions a few years ago when I heard of it and a guy I was dating had the 1e books. Never got to play it and still haven't, but I bought pretty hard into 2e. I have the text version of Origin and Heroes now, but I'm really anxious about how to start learning. See, I have played a fair bit of D&amp;D, but never DMed. D&amp;D really didn't seem hard to understand and I got the mechanics pretty easily and remember it. I haven't played anything White Wolf/D10 based and the whole Scions thing seems VERY daunting. I am having to DM this game when it comes out as it's the only way I could get people to play. Are there any tips/ advice of things to look at or watch? Is 1e similar enough to 2e that I could watch gameplay videos to get an idea? Any help is appreciated.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24047-jomack","name":"Jomack","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-03-30T16:28:35-07:00","id":1201459,"title":"","content":"Personally, I think a system that runs entirely on D10's is much easier to keep track of than one where you need D4's, D6's, D8's, D10's, D12's and D20's to manage all your character's actions.<br /> <br /> If you're just starting out, I would recommend limiting your chronicle to maybe one pantheon, with a preference to one that you are at least somewhat knowledgeable about. Unlike D&amp;D, this game is derived from real world mythology, and many of these pantheons are still worshiped even to this day. Not knowing how to accurately portray the religions and their associated cultures may offend some players.<br /> <br /> Ragnarok is the only book from 1E that I don't have, but I believe that it goes with the setting that only the Norse pantheon exists. Which makes it easier to explain why the other pantheons are mysteriously absent while the Giants are destroying the World.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","name":"Nyrufa","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6374"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-03-30T17:13:59-07:00","id":1201473,"title":"","content":"I say read the book, it helps if some of your other players read the book as well so you can help each other with the rules, and just start playing. You will quickly find out what the difficulties and rules you don't understand are. Then start looking up answers and asking questions on the forums.<br /> <br /> And having been introduced into Table Top RPGs by whitewolf and the D10 systems I have different expectations. So I always do a Session 0 tutorial where we do vignettes and snippets of each characters past. I try to link the PC's histories, this is infinitely helpful in playing the game as different characters could have little reason to hang out and it could feel contrived.<br /> <br /> But Play and find the flaws, this is what I did last time I learned a New system with Exalted 3rd ed. Nice simple advice.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/754-eldagusto","name":"Eldagusto","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar754_2.gif","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6435"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-03-30T18:33:49-07:00","id":1201497,"title":"","content":"<ul><li><b>Storytelling is a collaborative effort.</b> That means <b>the players share a responsibility to actively participate.</b> By this, I don't mean some fun-police thing about shaming players for looking at their phones or not being proactive enough. I mean instead that the Storyteller shouldn't be expected to carry the full burden of imagining the game and sketching the trajectory of the story. There are things the players can do to help you develop the setting; invite and ask them to do so. Before you begin play, ask your players: what kind of game are you interested in playing? What's interesting about Scion and what's not so interesting? Is there a particular focus you can agree on as tying everybody's play experience together? Do you have an interest in facing particular kinds of conflicts or antagonists? Do you want a rollicking action comedy or an emotional mythic tale? Mine your players for ideas. You're not just there to inspire them. They're also there to inspire you.</li> <li><b>Designing characters is part of that collaboration.</b> Don't just ask your players to come up with ideas separately and show up with disparate character sheets and then back-fill. Talk shop until you and your players share a pitch of who the players are as a group, what binds them together both conceptually and in practice in the World. Ask your players to create characters who complement one another and will either get along, or if they clash, clash in a way that's fun and doesn't disrupt the game.</li> <li><b>Prepare, but don't expect.</b> No plan survives contact with the enemy, and while the role of a player isn't one of an enemy, they're just as unpredictable. When you come up with setting elements, like non-player characters who play a role in the characters' drama, it's often a good idea to have at least some brief notes in your head on what role they're there to play out, what they're good for dramatically, and what they might be doing if the players don't interfere (as well as how they might likely respond if the players do). However, never make ironclad preparations, and don't expect anything the NPCs do to be inevitable. Players have creative ideas; they goof around; they swerve in unexpected directions. Expect to do some ad-libbing and improvise on the spot. When the players have ideas, run with them.</li> </ul>The Scion 2e material has some strong advice on what kind of stories and twists are good for a game of Scion. It also will walk you through what kind of writeup you should have ready for non-player characters who enter into conflict with the PCs, with several examples ranging in specificity. You could have some of the more generic writeups, like SWAT officers or Men in Black, on hand even when they don't figure into your direct expectations, just in case things turn sideways.<br /> <br /> Scion 1e's Storytelling Adventure System is <i>very</i> different from Scion 2e's Storypath System, and the tone of the 1e material is rather different as well. Gameplay of Scion 1e might be useful, but about as useful as most other games that aren't in the D&amp;D genre of dungeoneering and questing, in terms of what it suggests about how to run a big open world in a modern society.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/522-stupid-loserman","name":"Stupid Loserman","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar522_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1824"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}